\viewkind4\uc1 \f1\fs20\b WORKSHEET\b0\par
\f1\fs32\b Pursuing Justice\b0\fs24\par
\f1\fs20 Torah: Deuteronomy 16:18\u8211?21:9 | Haftarah: Isaiah 51:12\u8211?52:12 | Gospel: John 14:9\u8211?20\par
\f0\fs22\i In Shoftim all authority \u8212? judge, king, prophet, priest \u8212? is given by God, and precisely therefore bounded and testable. Around the image of the king who copies out the Torah by his own hand, \u8216?that his heart may not be lifted up,\u8217? the reading moves toward a God who protects life even in war, and toward Yeshua as the authority that does \u8216?not speak from Himself.\u8217?\i0\par
\par\f0\fs24 Work from top to bottom: first observe (A1), then weigh (A2), then
diagnose (A3), and only then summarize (A4). Observation precedes
interpretation, so you do not read into the text what you draw from it
moments later. Below is a fully filled-in example for Shoftim; use it as
a model, and dare to leave the open questions and anomalies open.

