\par\b\fs28 A2. Connecting hypotheses\b0\fs24\par

\u8594? Formulate two readings the text could carry, and choose with
the test: which explains the most data with the least force? Also name
what could refute your reading.
\b Hypothesis 1 (one axis):\b0  the center of gravity is
the Source axis alone \u8212? all authority comes from God (king, prophet,
priest). The limits are then merely an outworking of that
legitimacy.
\b Hypothesis 2 (relation):\b0  the center of gravity is
the relation between Source and Coherence \u8212? authority is given, AND
precisely therefore bounded, testable, and accountable.
\b Which explains the most data with the least force?\b0 
Hypothesis 2. Hypothesis 1 explains the source, but makes the
overwhelming emphasis on limits (king-under-the-Torah, prophet test,
witnesses, proportionality, the fruit tree) a side issue \u8212? that costs
force. Hypothesis 2 explains both, and the text stages the coupling
itself: 17:18\u8211?20 binds the source (God chooses the king) to the limit
(he writes out the Torah), and 18:15\u8211?22 binds the prophet\u8217?s legitimacy
to the test.
\b What would refute this reading?\b0  If 17:18\u8211?20 and
18:15\u8211?22 turned out not to be structural hinges but loose rules, the
relation would be imposed. Honest limit: hypothesis 2 risks a too-neat
synthesis; the safeguard is that those verses name source AND limit in
one breath.

